1. Unique Binary Search Trees II – LeetCode 95

Concepts: Recursion, BST, Divide and Conquer

class Solution {
    public List<TreeNode> generateTrees(int n) {
        if (n == 0) return new ArrayList<>();
        return build(1, n);
    }

    private List<TreeNode> build(int start, int end) {
        List<TreeNode> result = new ArrayList<>();

        if (start > end) {
            result.add(null);
            return result;
        }

        for (int i = start; i <= end; i++) {
            List<TreeNode> left = build(start, i - 1);
            List<TreeNode> right = build(i + 1, end);

            for (TreeNode l : left) {
                for (TreeNode r : right) {
                    TreeNode root = new TreeNode(i);
                    root.left = l;
                    root.right = r;
                    result.add(root);
                }
            }
        }
        return result;
    }
}

Approach:

Choose each number as the root.

Recursively generate all possible left subtrees and right subtrees.

Combine every left and right subtree with the selected root.

Store all unique BSTs in the result.

Time Complexity: Catalan-number growth (exponential output size).

Space Complexity: Depends on the generated trees and recursion.

2. Unique Binary Search Trees – LeetCode 96

Concepts: Dynamic Programming, Catalan Numbers

class Solution {
    public int numTrees(int n) {
        int[] dp = new int[n + 1];
        dp[0] = 1;
        dp[1] = 1;

        for (int nodes = 2; nodes <= n; nodes++) {
            for (int root = 1; root <= nodes; root++) {
                dp[nodes] += dp[root - 1] * dp[nodes - root];
            }
        }

        return dp[n];
    }
}

Approach:

dp[i] represents the number of unique BSTs with i nodes.

Each number can be selected as the root.

Calculate the combinations of left and right subtrees.

Store the results using Dynamic Programming.

Time Complexity: O(n²)

Space Complexity: O(n)

3. Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree – LeetCode 297

Concepts: Binary Tree, DFS, Recursion, Serialization

public class Codec {
    public String serialize(TreeNode root) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        preorder(root, sb);
        return sb.toString();
    }

    private void preorder(TreeNode node, StringBuilder sb) {
        if (node == null) {
            sb.append("#,");
            return;
        }

        sb.append(node.val).append(",");
        preorder(node.left, sb);
        preorder(node.right, sb);
    }

    public TreeNode deserialize(String data) {
        Queue<String> queue = new LinkedList<>(
            Arrays.asList(data.split(",", -1))
        );
        return build(queue);
    }

    private TreeNode build(Queue<String> queue) {
        String value = queue.poll();

        if (value.equals("#")) return null;

        TreeNode node = new TreeNode(Integer.parseInt(value));
        node.left = build(queue);
        node.right = build(queue);

        return node;
    }
}

Approach:

Serialize the tree using preorder traversal.

Use # to represent null nodes.

During deserialization, read each value from the queue.

Reconstruct the tree recursively.

Time Complexity: O(n) for serialization and deserialization.

Space Complexity: O(n)

4. Construct BST from Preorder Traversal – LeetCode 1008

Concepts: BST, Recursion, Preorder Traversal

class Solution {
    private int index = 0;

    public TreeNode bstFromPreorder(int[] preorder) {
        index = 0;
        return build(preorder, Long.MAX_VALUE);
    }

    private TreeNode build(int[] preorder, long bound) {
        if (index == preorder.length || preorder[index] > bound) {
            return null;
        }

        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(preorder[index++]);

        root.left = build(preorder, root.val);
        root.right = build(preorder, bound);

        return root;
    }
}

Approach:

Use preorder traversal to construct the BST.

Maintain an upper bound for each subtree.

Construct the left subtree with the root value as the upper bound.

Construct the right subtree with the inherited upper bound.

Time Complexity: O(n)

Space Complexity: O(h), where h is the height of the tree.

5. Convert BST to Greater Tree – LeetCode 538

Concepts: BST, Reverse Inorder Traversal, Recursion

class Solution {
    private int sum = 0;

    public TreeNode convertBST(TreeNode root) {
        sum = 0;
        reverseInorder(root);
        return root;
    }

    private void reverseInorder(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) return;

        reverseInorder(node.right);

        sum += node.val;
        node.val = sum;

        reverseInorder(node.left);
    }
}

Approach:
Perform reverse inorder traversal (Right → Root → Left).
Maintain a running sum.
Add the current node's value to the running sum.
Update the node with the accumulated sum.
Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(h)

